  "notes": "The defense starts by cashing 3 rounds of hearts. West leads a fourth round of hearts and East ruffs, killing North's heart winner.\n\nSouth should overruff with a trump other than the ♠4. If South overruffs with the ♠4, he will never be able to reach dummy in spades. After ruffing high and drawing trump, South must decide how to get rid of his diamond losers.\n\nIf West has the ♦K, he can be finessed for that card. South can later trump his third diamond in the dummy. It is very unlikely that West has the ♦K. West passed originally but has already shown up with 9 points in hearts and a singleton in spades. If West also had the ♦K, he almost certainly would have opened the bidding.\n\nBy similar thinking, the inference is even stronger that East has the ♣A. Declarer should plan on taking a ruffing finesse against that card. South should play the carefully preserved ♠4 to the dummy and lead clubs through East.\n\nIf East plays low, South can discard a diamond and repeat the play in clubs. Whenever East plays the ♣A, South ruffs and crosses to the ♦A. Dummy's now good clubs can be used to discard whatever diamond losers South has left.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "The defense starts by cashing 3 rounds of hearts. West leads a fourth round of hearts and East ruffs, killing North's heart winner.",
    "South should overruff with a trump other than the ♠4. If South overruffs with the ♠4, he will never be able to reach dummy in spades. After ruffing high and drawing trump, South must decide how to get rid of his diamond losers.",
    "If West has the ♦K, he can be finessed for that card. South can later trump his third diamond in the dummy. It is very unlikely that West has the ♦K. West passed originally but has already shown up with 9 points in hearts and a singleton in spades. If West also had the ♦K, he almost certainly would have opened the bidding.",
    "By similar thinking, the inference is even stronger that East has the ♣A. Declarer should plan on taking a ruffing finesse against that card. South should play the carefully preserved ♠4 to the dummy and lead clubs through East.",
    "If East plays low, South can discard a diamond and repeat the play in clubs. Whenever East plays the ♣A, South ruffs and crosses to the ♦A. Dummy's now good clubs can be used to discard whatever diamond losers South has left."
